      {
        "t": "chr",
        "v": [
          "This module classifies repository files and, for tabular data files available locally, extracts each column's type and summary statistics.",
          "#### Data Tree",
          "Where the files sit within each repository. Each file is followed by how it was classified, which study it belongs to.",
          "**Repository: https://osf.io/trial**",
          "```\n└── data/\n    ├── demographics.csv  📊 data ex1\n    ├── sub-01.csv        📊 data ex1\n    └── sub-02.csv        📊 data ex1\n```",
          "#### Data Files",
          "::: {.panel-tabset}\n\n## demographics.csv\n\n\n```{r}\n#| echo: false\n\n\n# table data --------------------------------------\ntable <- structure(list(id = 1:4, age = c(21L, 34L, 28L, 45L)), row.names = c(NA, \n4L), class = \"data.frame\")\n\n# display table -----------------------------------\nmetacheck::report_table(table, \"auto\", 10, FALSE)\n```\n\n\n:::",
          "#### Descriptives Overview\n\nWe found 0 columns with at least one missing value.",
          "::: {.panel-tabset}\n\n## demographics.csv\n\n\n```{r}\n#| echo: false\n\n\n# table data --------------------------------------\ntable <- structure(list(Column = c(\"age\", \"id\"), Representation = c(\"numeric\", \n\"text\"), Level = c(\"ratio\", \"nominal\"), Concept = c(\"age\", \"id\"\n), Role = c(\"measure\", \"identifier\"), Unit = c(\"years\", \"\"), \n    Rows = c(4L, 4L), \"% Missing\" = c(0, 0), \"N Unique\" = c(4L, \n    4L), Mean = c(32, NA), SD = c(10.165, NA), Min = c(21, NA\n    ), Max = c(45, NA)), row.names = c(\"age\", \"id\"), class = \"data.frame\")\n\n# display table -----------------------------------\nmetacheck::report_table(table, \"auto\", 25, FALSE)\n```\n\n\n:::",
          "This module runs automated data-quality checks (outliers, miscoded missing values, empty and constant columns, SPSS filter variables, category casing, problematic column names, mixed text encodings, spreadsheet formatting) on the extracted data files.",
          "We examined 2 columns across 1 data file.",
          "#### Careless Responding",
          "No file could be screened for careless responding.",
          "**What this does not cover.** Only 0 of 1 data file could be screened: the rest had no detectable multi-item scale block, or too few respondents. Even in a screened file, these checks only look at multi-item rating scales — the only place where a repeated or flat answer pattern is interpretable. Single questions, open text, demographics and any item outside a detected scale block are never examined. A respondent can therefore answer most of a survey carelessly and still not be flagged here, and finding nothing is not evidence that a dataset is free of careless responding."
        ]
      },
      {
        "t": "chr",
        "v": [
          "green"
        ]
      },
      {
        "t": "chr",
        "v": [
          "\n-  We classified 3 files: 3 data.\n-  We found 1 tabular data file and extracted 2 columns from 1 of them.\n-  We grouped the files into 1 study group (ex1).\n-  The study-group step did not get an answer for 3 files (the model request failed); they were grouped by the fallback rules instead.\n-  2 trial-level data files (E-Prime / Inquisit / jsPsych / Behaverse) were recognised. These are per-participant records of a behavioural task, so they are not listed as separate datasets — convert_psychds() merges them per instrument into Behaverse `paradata/<instrument>.json` (one file per instrument, all participants). Nothing is deleted. We ran automated data-quality checks on 2 columns and found no issues. We detected demographic column for Age."
        ]
